Now I see the full picture. In `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string`:

```c
if (oentry->asn_desc != NULL) { /* complex */
    ret = decode_complex_string(oentry, value, value_size, &tmp);
    if (ret < 0) {
        goto unknown_oid;
    }
} else {
    ret = _gnutls_x509_decode_string(oentry->etype, value,
                                     value_size, &tmp, 0);
    if (ret < 0) {
        goto unknown_oid;
    }
}

ret = str_escape(&tmp, str);
_gnutls_free_datum(&tmp);
```

When `decode_complex_string` succeeds, `tmp` is properly set. Then `str_escape` is called with `&tmp`. `str_escape` reads `tmp->data` and `tmp->size`.

But wait - what if `decode_complex_string` succeeds but `tmp` has a NUL byte in the middle? Then `str_escape` would read past the NUL byte. But that's not an uninitialized value issue.

Let me look at `str_escape` again:

```c
for (i = j = 0; i < str->size; i++) {
    if (str->data[i] == 0) {
        ret = gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_ASN1_DER_ERROR);
        goto cleanup;
    }
    ...
}
```

If `str->data[i]` is 0 (NUL), it returns an error. But this is a read of `str->data[i]`, which is initialized (it's part of the allocated buffer).

OK, I think I need to look at this from the perspective of what MSAN actually detects. MSAN detects when an uninitialized value is used in a way that affects program behavior. The key is that the value must be uninitialized (not just zero or garbage).

Let me look at the `_gnutls_x509_read_value` function again:

```c
x509_read_value(ASN1_TYPE c, const char *root,
                gnutls_datum_t * ret, unsigned allow_null)
{
    int len = 0, result;
    uint8_t *tmp = NULL;
    unsigned int etype;

    result = asn1_read_value_type(c, root, NULL, &len, &etype);
    ...
    tmp = gnutls_malloc((size_t) len + 1);
    ...
    if (len > 0) {
        result = asn1_read_value(c, root, tmp, &len);
        ...
        ret->size = (unsigned) len;
    } else {
        ret->size = 0;
    }

    tmp[ret->size] = 0;
    ret->data = tmp;
    ...
}
```

When `len > 0`, `asn1_read_value` fills `tmp` with data and sets `len` to the actual length. Then `ret->size = len` and `tmp[ret->size] = 0`.

When `len == 0`, `ret->size = 0` and `tmp[0] = 0`. `ret->data = tmp` (which is a 1-byte buffer with just a NUL).

This seems fine.

